{"qid":"Q155019","names":["Duchy of Lorraine"],"title":"Duchy of Lorraine","endonym":{"native":"Duché de Lorraine","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"The Duchy of Lorraine derived from the Upper Lotharingia left after the partition of Charlemagne's empire, formally constituted as a distinct duchy within the Holy Roman Empire in 959. It passed through numerous dynasties, including the House of Alsace and later the House of Vaudémont, before Francis Stephen of the House of Lorraine exchanged it for Tuscany in 1737 — receiving Tuscany as compensation — whereupon it was assigned to the deposed Polish king Stanisław Leszczyński as a life fief. On Stanisław's death in 1766 the duchy reverted to France, ending over eight centuries of distinct political existence with Nancy as its principal seat.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Lorraine's boundaries are well documented through Holy Roman Empire records and French royal correspondence; internal changes across centuries are broadly traceable.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Duchy of Lorraine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Lorraine"},{"label":"Britannica: Lorraine","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Lorraine-historical-region-France"},{"label":"Britannica: Stanisław I","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Stanislaw-I-king-of-Poland"}],"britannica":"place/Upper-Lorraine","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Duchy of Lorraine","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"René I of Anjou","nativeName":"René Ier d'Anjou","roman":"","start":1431,"end":1453,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"John II, Duke of Lorraine","nativeN